course: dictionary
lesson: 825
title: Bellow: a loud, deep voice
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A bellow is a loud, deep shout or roar. Context clues help you spot which meaning is used. «LIGHTS OFF, YOU TWO,» Maddie's dad bellows up the stairs. The capital letters and the words up the stairs show that he is shouting, and loudly, so bellows means shouts in a very loud, deep voice.
explain: Animals can bellow too. «Alligators bellow so deeply that the water on their backs dances.» The word deeply tells you the roar is low and strong. Bulls, stags and elephants can bellow as well.
explain: Bellows with an s can be a tool, not a sound. A bellows pumps air into a fire to make it burn hotter, and it is found by old fireplaces and in a blacksmith's forge. Ask yourself: is this about a voice or about a fire? The clue tells you which meaning to use.
fact: Bellow means to shout or roar in a very loud, deep voice.
fact: Bellow can be a verb, as in «he bellowed», or a noun, as in «a bellow».
fact: Bellows is always plural, even when it means one tool: a tool that pumps air into a fire to make it burn hotter.
fact: Writers often use «bellowed» instead of «said» to show a big, loud, often angry character.
fact: Bulls, stags and elephants are animals that can bellow in nature writing.
exercise: «LIGHTS OFF, YOU TWO,» Maddie's dad bellows up the stairs. What does bellows mean here? | *Shouts in a very loud, deep voice | Pumps air into a fire | Sings a happy tune | Whispers softly to the children
exercise: A blacksmith works the bellows in the forge. What are the bellows? | A small, quiet wind | A loud, deep roar from a bull | A big, angry shout | *A tool that pumps air into a fire to make it burn hotter
exercise: The bull gave a loud bellow across the field. What is a bellow here? | A small stone | *A loud, deep roar | A long walk | A soft tune
test: «Alligators bellow so deeply that the water on their backs dances.» In this sentence, what does bellow mean? | A small splash | A happy song | *A loud, deep roar | A quiet hum
test: «'Get out!' bellowed the giant.» Why might an author write bellowed instead of said? | To show the giant is very small | To show the giant is asleep | It means exactly the same as said, so there is no reason | *To show a big, loud, often angry voice
exam: «The bellows made the fire burn hotter.» What does bellows mean in this sentence? | A roar from an animal | A large wooden box for toys | A loud, deep shout | *A tool that pumps air into a fire
